# breath.gen -- the modules that shape sound, shaping the composition.
#
# Every other piece here composes with composers: plugins that emit notes
# and plugins that change them. This one has two oscillators and an
# envelope in its chains, and not one of them makes a sound. They are the
# same `osc::simple' and `env::adsr' a patch is built out of -- the same
# .so files, wired the same way with the same arrow -- run fifty times a
# second on the composer's side of the program, where what they modulate
# is the music rather than the audio.
#
# UNIFICATION.md phase 3, and the argument is that a shape over time does
# not care what it is a shape of. `freq = 0.05' is a twenty-second cycle
# here and a very low note in a patch, and osc::simple cannot tell: it
# divides by whatever rate it is being run at, which is the whole reason
# one plugin can serve both. So a composer inherits the DSP toolkit
# rather than growing a second, worse copy of it -- and if you want a
# triangle instead of a sine, that is `waveform = 2', which you already
# knew.
#
# A NODE IS NOT A STAGE, even though both are spelled `stage'. Events
# never flow through `lfo'; nothing is passed to it and nothing comes out
# the far side. It holds a number, and the composer stages read that
# number with `->' at the moment they want it. That is why a chain full
# of nodes still needs a generator in it, and why the arrow points the
# way it does.
#
# WHY THE ARITHMETIC IS SPELLED OUT. An oscillator runs -1 to +1, and
# `prob' wants 0 to 1. Nothing converts between them, on purpose: `mul'
# and `add' are what a patch would use and they are right here. It is
# three lines instead of one, and in exchange there is no hidden mapping
# to remember and no second meaning for the arrow depending on which side
# it lands on. The Depth knob is what `mul' multiplies by, so it is the
# depth of the breathing; the `0.5' that `add' lifts by is what it
# breathes around.
#
# WHAT NODES MAY NOT DO. `delay' and `fft' count in samples, and a sample
# down here is a fiftieth of a second rather than twenty microseconds --
# a 4410-sample delay is a hundred milliseconds on the audio thread and a
# minute and a half on this one. They are refused by name rather than
# left to produce numbers nobody meant. So is `osc::static', for a
# different reason: it draws from the global random generator, and a
# piece that used it would not replay.
#
# The nodes run on transport time like everything else. Pause and the
# breathing stops where it is; rewind and it starts again from the top,
# byte for byte -- gencheck renders this file twice and diffs it, which
# is the same gate every other piece here passes and the reason a node
# had to be as repeatable as a composer before it was allowed in.

name "Breath";
author "Misha Nasledov";
description "An LFO breathing a chain's density, and an envelope over four minutes.";

seed 3141;

@depth = 0.45;
@depth.widget = 1;
@depth.min = 0;
@depth.max = 0.5;
@depth.label = "Depth";

scale amin "A2 C3 E3 A3 B3 C4 E4 G4 A4";

instrument pad {
    dsp  "amb01.dsp";

    a    = 600 ms;
    d    = 1400 ms;
    s    = 0.8;
    r    = 3500 ms;

    res  = 0.65;
    fmin = 0.05;
    fmax = 0.6;
};

# The breathing itself: a twenty-second cycle, ten seconds opening and
# ten closing, with the line speaking more often at the top of it than at
# the bottom.
#
# `lfo' is an oscillator whose entire cycle is slower than most of the
# notes it governs -- which is the only thing separating it from one you
# would hear. `half' and `mid' put its -1..+1 where prob's 0..1 is: the
# depth is a knob, so the breathing can be flattened to nothing or opened
# to its full swing while the piece plays.
chain breathing {
    stage lfo  osc::simple { freq = 0.05; waveform = 0; amp = 1; };
    stage half math::mul   { in0 = lfo->out;  in1 = @depth; };
    stage mid  math::add   { in0 = half->out; in1 = 0.5; };

    stage src gen::eno_line {
        notes = amin;  period = 2.7 s;  jitter = 0.6 s;
        prob = mid->out;  hold = 5 s;  vel = 62;  vel_jitter = 8;
    };

    sink { instrument = pad; };
};

# The other half of the deliverable: an envelope shaping the piece over
# minutes rather than milliseconds -- four and a half of them, thirty
# seconds opening and four more settling.
#
# adsr's durations are in samples, and a sample here is a fiftieth of a
# second -- so 1500 is thirty seconds of attack and 12000 is four minutes
# of decay. That is the arithmetic being at a different rate, stated
# plainly; it is also the reason `delay' is refused, since a plugin whose
# numbers mean *time* rather than *a count of windows* would be silently
# wrong instead of usefully strange.
#
# `trigger = 1' holds it open, so the envelope runs its attack and decay
# once and then sits on its sustain: the low voice comes in over half a
# minute, thins out over the next four, and stays where it settles. `r'
# is written for completeness and never reached, because nothing here
# ever lets go of the trigger.
chain arc {
    stage env  env::adsr { a = 1500; d = 12000; s = 0.35; r = 6000;
                           trigger = 1; };
    stage lift math::mul { in0 = env->out; in1 = 0.5; };
    stage base math::add { in0 = lift->out; in1 = 0.35; };

    stage low gen::eno_line {
        notes = "A2 E3";  period = 6.1 s;  jitter = 1.4 s;
        prob = base->out;  hold = 9 s;  vel = 54;  vel_jitter = 6;
    };

    sink { instrument = pad; };
};

# And one chain with no nodes in it at all, so the piece has something
# that does not breathe to breathe against.
chain steady {
    stage src gen::eno_line {
        notes = "C4 E4 G4";  period = 11.3 s;  jitter = 3 s;
        prob = 0.5;  hold = 7 s;  vel = 46;  vel_jitter = 8;
    };
    sink { instrument = pad; };
};
